)|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -8 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+8)=0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}(\\lambda +1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ (double root) and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1$ (simple root).\n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda =-1.$ Since ${V}_{-1}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda =-3,$ which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of ${V}_{-3},$ we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-3)I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -10 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-3})=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96ba342a-7051-5aec-bb1e-1e67c17f68b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a573b5ad-12de-5838-9555-2e6712ed23a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186488203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488325 #edb/ref 1062 #uu